From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Tobias Koch <tobias.koch@aeltra.eu>
Date: Sat, 8 Aug 2026 16:21:43 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] libfetch: fix use-after-free in fetch_cache_put()

Commit d430a989761e ("libfetch: fix use-after-free in connection cache
management") hoisted next_cached out of the eviction loop so that the
walk no longer reads iter after the close callback has freed it.  The
same increment expression still advances "last", however, and it runs
for entries that were just unlinked and handed to that callback.

"last" is therefore left pointing at freed memory, and the next
eviction writes through it via "last->next_cached = ...", corrupting
the connection cache.

Advance "last" only for entries that are retained.

Signed-off-by: Tobias Koch <tobias.koch@aeltra.eu>
---
 libfetch/common.c | 6 ++++--
 1 file changed, 4 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/libfetch/common.c b/libfetch/common.c
index 1f2d0cd..b1ca550 100644
--- a/libfetch/common.c
+++ b/libfetch/common.c
@@ -375,14 +375,16 @@ fetch_cache_put(conn_t *conn, int (*closecb)(conn_t *))
 
 	global_count = host_count = 0;
 	last = NULL;
-	for (iter = connection_cache; iter; last = iter, iter = next_cached) {
+	for (iter = connection_cache; iter; iter = next_cached) {
 		next_cached = iter->next_cached;
 		++global_count;
 		if (strcmp(conn->cache_url->host, iter->cache_url->host) == 0)
 			++host_count;
 		if (global_count < cache_global_limit &&
-		    host_count < cache_per_host_limit)
+		    host_count < cache_per_host_limit) {
+			last = iter;
 			continue;
+		}
 		--global_count;
 		if (last != NULL)
 			last->next_cached = iter->next_cached;
